There are no data on the presence of dalfampridine in human milk, the effects on the breastfed infant, or the effects on milk production. The developmental and health benefits of breastfeeding should be considered along with the mother’s clinical need for therapy and any potential adverse effects on the breastfed infant.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'Dalfampridine is a broad-spectrum potassium channel blocker that improves action potential conduction in demyelinated nerve fibers.', '["Dalfampridine blocks voltage-gated potassium channels exposed in demyelinated axons of the central nervous system.", "This blockade delays repolarization and prolongs the duration of the action potential.", "The prolonged action potential overcomes the conduction block caused by demyelination.", "This enhances the conduction of action potentials across demyelinated neural pathways.", "The improved neural transmission translates clinically to improved walking speed and neurological function in patients with multiple sclerosis."]', '**OCT2 Inhibitors (e.g., Cimetidine):**
