It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk and due to the potential for adverse anticholinergic effects in the nursing infant, caution should be exercised, and use is generally not recommended.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'Homatropine is a competitive muscarinic receptor antagonist that blocks cholinergic stimulation of the iris sphincter and ciliary muscles, resulting in pupillary dilation (mydriasis) and paralysis of accommodation (cycloplegia).', '["Homatropine acts as a competitive antagonist at muscarinic acetylcholine receptors in the eye.", "It blocks the responses of the iris sphincter muscle to cholinergic stimulation, causing the pupil to dilate (mydriasis).", "It also blocks the cholinergic stimulation of the accommodative ciliary muscle, leading to paralysis of accommodation (cycloplegia).", "These effects facilitate thorough examination of the retina and relieve ciliary spasm in inflammatory conditions like uveitis."]', '**Other Anticholinergic Agents (e.g., Antihistamines, TCAs, Phenothiazines):**
