It is not known whether L-ornithine L-aspartate is excreted in human milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'L-Ornithine L-Aspartate lowers elevated blood ammonia levels by stimulating the urea cycle and glutamine synthesis in the liver and peripheral tissues.', '["Following administration, the compound dissociates into the endogenous amino acids L-ornithine and L-aspartate.", "L-ornithine acts as an activator and substrate for the enzymes ornithine carbamoyltransferase and carbamoyl phosphate synthetase in the hepatic urea cycle, facilitating the conversion of toxic ammonia into non-toxic urea.", "L-aspartate acts as a substrate for the enzyme glutamine synthetase in both hepatic and extrahepatic tissues (e.g., skeletal muscle), promoting the binding of ammonia to glutamate to form glutamine.", "These combined actions significantly reduce circulating ammonia levels, alleviating the neurological symptoms of hepatic encephalopathy and supporting liver cell regeneration."]', 'No clinically significant drug interactions have been reported. However, caution is advised when co-administering with other drugs that significantly alter renal function or urea excretion.', '**Hepatic Encephalopathy and Hyperammonemia**
- *Intravenous (Infusion Concentrate):* Up to 20 g (4 ampoules) daily. In cases of pre-coma or coma, up to 8 ampoules within 24 hours may be given, depending on the severity of the condition.
