Safety and efficacy have not been established in females. There are no data on the presence of the drug in human milk. It is contraindicated in nursing mothers.', '{"trimester_1": "Contraindicated", "trimester_2": "Contraindicated", "trimester_3": "Contraindicated"}', 'Degarelix rapidly reduces testosterone levels by blocking the receptors in the pituitary gland that signal the testicles to produce testosterone, starving prostate cancer cells of the hormones they need to grow.', '["Degarelix acts as a selective, competitive gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) receptor antagonist.", "It reversibly binds to GnRH receptors in the anterior pituitary gland.", "This binding blocks endogenous GnRH from activating the receptors, rapidly inhibiting the secretion of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH).", "The sharp decline in LH leads to a rapid and profound decrease in testosterone production by the testes, achieving medical castration levels (<0.5 ng/mL) within days.", "Unlike GnRH agonists (e.g., leuprolide), degarelix does not cause an initial surge in LH and testosterone, thereby preventing the clinical ''flare'' of prostate cancer symptoms at the start of therapy."]', '- **QT Prolonging Drugs (e.g., Class IA/III Antiarrhythmics, Antipsychotics, Methadone, Moxifloxacin):** Concurrent use increases the risk of significant QT interval prolongation and Torsades de Pointes. **Management:** Avoid concurrent use if possible. If necessary, monitor ECG and electrolytes closely.
