### Lactation
It is not known whether this drug is excreted in human milk. Because many drugs are excreted in human milk, caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'Fibrin sealant mimics the final step of the physiological coagulation cascade to form a stable fibrin clot that adheres to tissues and achieves hemostasis.', '["Upon mixing the two components (Sealer Protein containing human fibrinogen and Thrombin), the thrombin rapidly cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin monomers.", "These fibrin monomers polymerize to form a soluble, rubber-like fibrin network.", "Endogenous Factor XIII (activated by thrombin to Factor XIIIa) cross-links the fibrin network, creating a stable, insoluble clot.", "Aprotinin, an antifibrinolytic agent included in the formulation, inhibits plasmin and delays the degradation of the fibrin clot, ensuring prolonged hemostasis and tissue sealing.", "The resulting mass adheres to the wound surface, achieving hemostasis and sealing tissues even in heparinized patients or those on anti-platelet drugs."]', '**Oxidized Cellulose Preparations:**
- *Clinical Result:* Oxidized cellulose-containing preparations can reduce the efficacy of fibrin sealants by interfering with the polymerization process due to their low pH.
- *Management:* Avoid concurrent use. If oxidized cellulose was used, thoroughly clean the area before applying the fibrin sealant.', '**Adjunct to Hemostasis & Sealing**
- *Maximum area 8 cm² (cannula) or 100 cm² (spray):* Use 2 mL.
- *Maximum area 16 cm² (cannula) or 200 cm² (spray):* Use 4 mL.
