No effects on the breastfed newborn/infant are anticipated since systemic exposure of the breastfeeding woman to topically applied fusidic acid is negligible. It is safe to use during lactation, but avoid application to the breast area to prevent accidental ingestion by the infant.', '{"trimester_1": "Safe", "trimester_2": "Safe", "trimester_3": "Safe"}', 'Fusidic acid is a bacteriostatic antibiotic that inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by preventing the translocation of the ribosome.', '["Fusidic acid penetrates the bacterial cell wall and binds to elongation factor G (EF-G).", "EF-G is an essential protein required for the translocation of the ribosome along the mRNA during protein synthesis.", "By binding to EF-G, fusidic acid prevents the release of the EF-G/GDP complex from the ribosome.", "This halts the translocation of the peptide subunits and prevents the elongation of the peptide chain.", "Bacterial protein synthesis is effectively inhibited, leading to a bacteriostatic effect against susceptible Gram-positive organisms."]', '- **Antistaphylococcal Penicillins:** Exhibits synergistic antibacterial action when used concurrently.
