- Systemic NSAID adverse effects (GI upset, renal impairment) are extremely rare due to minimal systemic absorption.', 'Data not available', '**Risk Summary:** Felbinac is suspected to cause serious birth defects when administered during pregnancy. As with other NSAIDs, it inhibits prostaglandin synthesis, which can cause premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and delay parturition. It is contraindicated in pregnancy.', 'Felbinac should not be used during breastfeeding. It is contraindicated during lactation.', '{"trimester_1": "Contraindicated", "trimester_2": "Contraindicated", "trimester_3": "Contraindicated"}', 'Felbinac is a topical non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) that reduces local pain and inflammation by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.', '["Felbinac penetrates the skin to reach the underlying inflamed tissues (muscles, tendons, joints).", "It directly and reversibly inhibits the cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.", "This blocks the conversion of arachidonic acid into pro-inflammatory prostaglandins and thromboxane.", "By lowering prostaglandin accumulation in periarticular and cutaneous tissues, it decreases nociceptor sensitization, dampening pain signaling and reducing vascular permeability and inflammatory edema."]', '**Moderate:**
- **Systemic Interactions:** Felbinac is highly protein-bound; however, serum levels following topical application are extremely low. Therefore, clinical drug interactions with systemic medications are unlikely.', '**Topical Gel/Foam (3%):**
- Apply 1 to 2 grams (approximately 1 to 2 inches of gel) to the affected site 2 to 4 times daily and rub in gently.
