Due to its minimal systemic absorption, clinically significant amounts of aluminum are not expected to be excreted in human breast milk. It is considered compatible with breastfeeding.', '{"trimester_1": "Safe", "trimester_2": "Safe", "trimester_3": "Safe"}', 'Neutralizes gastric acid and binds dietary phosphate in the gastrointestinal tract.', '["Reacts chemically with hydrochloric acid in the stomach to form aluminum chloride and water.", "This neutralization process increases gastric pH, which subsequently inhibits the proteolytic activity of pepsin.", "In the intestinal tract, aluminum ions bind to dietary phosphate to form insoluble aluminum phosphate.", "The insoluble aluminum phosphate complex is then excreted in the feces, effectively lowering serum phosphate levels."]', '- **Tetracyclines, Quinolones, Cephalosporins, Bisphosphonates, Isoniazid, Iron Salts, Levothyroxine:** Aluminum hydroxide binds to these drugs in the GI tract, significantly decreasing their absorption and clinical efficacy. **Management:** Separate administration by at least 2 to 3 hours.
