### Lactation
It is not known whether topical application of sulfur and salicylic acid results in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in breast milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman. Do not apply to the breast area prior to nursing to avoid accidental ingestion by the infant.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'This combination provides a synergistic keratolytic effect; salicylic acid desquamates the stratum corneum while sulfur acts as a mild antiseptic, scabicide, and keratolytic agent.', '["Salicylic acid acts as a desmolytic agent, breaking down the intercellular cement (desmosomes) between corneocytes in the stratum corneum.", "This action promotes the shedding of dead skin cells, unclogs pores, and reduces hyperkeratosis.", "Sulfur interacts with cysteine in the stratum corneum to form hydrogen sulfide, which exerts mild bacteriostatic and fungicidal properties.", "Sulfur also promotes keratolysis by softening the epidermis.", "Together, they synergistically reduce comedone formation, decrease inflammation, and normalize epidermal shedding in acne and seborrheic conditions."]', '- **Other Topical Acne Agents (e.g., Benzoyl Peroxide, Retinoids):** [Moderate] Increased risk of severe skin dryness, peeling, and irritation. Stagger application times if concurrent use is necessary.
