- **Oral (Syrup):** 1 to 2 teaspoonfuls (5 to 10 mL) three times a day.', '**Vitamin Deficiency**
- **Oral (Syrup):** 1 teaspoonful (5 mL) three times a day.', '[]', 'No dosage adjustment is necessary. Water-soluble vitamins are readily excreted by the kidneys.', 'Administer orally. May be taken with or without food. Taking with meals may reduce mild gastrointestinal upset. Shake the syrup well before use.', 'Overdose is rare as excess water-soluble vitamins are excreted in the urine. Massive acute ingestion may cause gastrointestinal distress. Chronic massive doses of pyridoxine (B6) can lead to severe sensory neuropathy and ataxia.', 'Treatment is primarily supportive. Discontinue the supplement. Symptoms of pyridoxine-induced neuropathy generally resolve slowly upon discontinuation of the vitamin.', 'Store below 30°C in a dry place. Protect from direct sunlight and light, as riboflavin is light-sensitive.', 'This medication may cause your urine to turn a bright yellow or orange color; this is a harmless effect of Vitamin B2 (riboflavin). Take it with food if it causes stomach upset. Do not exceed the recommended dose, as excessive amounts of Vitamin B6 over a long period can cause nerve damage.', '("Thiamine"[Mesh] OR "Vitamin B1"[tiab]) AND ("Riboflavin"[Mesh] OR "Vitamin B2"[tiab]) AND ("Vitamin B 6"[Mesh] OR "Vitamin B6"[tiab]) AND ("Niacinamide"[Mesh] OR "Nicotinamide"[tiab])');
- **Gastric Retention:** Contraindicated as the drug decreases gastrointestinal motility, which can worsen severe gastric retention or obstructive bowel disease.
