Pemirolast is excreted in the milk of lactating rats following oral administration. It is not known whether topical ocular administration results in sufficient systemic absorption to produce detectable quantities in human breast milk. Caution should be exercised when administered to a nursing woman.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'Pemirolast is a mast cell stabilizer that prevents the release of inflammatory mediators like histamine and leukotrienes by inhibiting calcium influx into mast cells.', '["Pemirolast acts locally on the ocular mucosa as a mast cell stabilizer.", "It inhibits the antigen-induced influx of calcium ions into mast cells.", "This prevents the degranulation of mast cells and the subsequent release of preformed inflammatory mediators (such as histamine) and newly synthesized mediators (such as leukotrienes and prostaglandins).", "It also inhibits the chemotaxis of eosinophils into ocular tissue, thereby suppressing the Type I immediate hypersensitivity reaction and relieving ocular itching."]', '- **Topical Ophthalmic Agents:** No specific drug interactions have been established. If using multiple topical ophthalmic medications, separate administration by at least 5 minutes to prevent washout of the previous drop.', '- **Prophylaxis and Treatment of Allergic Conjunctivitis:**
