It is not known whether tioconazole is excreted in human milk following vaginal administration. Because systemic absorption is low, the risk to the infant is likely minimal, but the manufacturer recommends caution or discontinuing breastfeeding temporarily.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Safe", "trimester_3": "Safe"}', 'Tioconazole is a broad-spectrum imidazole antifungal that inhibits the synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal cell membrane.', '["Tioconazole penetrates the fungal cell and inhibits the cytochrome P450-dependent enzyme 14-alpha-demethylase.", "This inhibition prevents the conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol.", "The depletion of ergosterol and the accumulation of toxic methylated sterols disrupt the structure and function of the fungal cell membrane.", "This leads to increased membrane permeability, leakage of intracellular contents, and ultimately fungal cell death."]', '- **Latex Contraceptives:** The ointment base contains mineral oil/petrolatum, which can weaken latex condoms and diaphragms, leading to contraceptive failure. Avoid concurrent use and for 3 days post-treatment.
