- **Premedication:** Acetaminophen and an antihistamine may be given to prevent fever and chills.', '- **Clinical Outcome:** Severe pulmonary toxicity (fibrosis, pneumonitis), profound mucositis, severe skin reactions (hyperpigmentation, ulceration), and fever.', '- **Management:** There is no specific antidote. Treatment is supportive. Monitor pulmonary function closely. Avoid the use of high-concentration oxygen, as it will exacerbate pulmonary toxicity. Corticosteroids may be considered for severe pneumonitis, though efficacy is not definitively proven.', '- Store intact vials in a refrigerator at 2°C to 8°C (36°F to 46°F).
- Reconstituted solutions are chemically stable for 24 hours at room temperature, but due to lack of preservatives, it is recommended to use within 24 hours of reconstitution.', '- Warn the patient about the risk of lung damage. Instruct them to report any shortness of breath, persistent cough, or wheezing immediately.
- Advise the patient that skin changes (darkening, peeling, rash) and mouth sores are very common. Good oral hygiene is essential.
