It is unknown whether elobixibat or its metabolites are excreted in human milk. Because systemic absorption of elobixibat is minimal, exposure to the breastfed infant is expected to be low. The developmental and health benefits of breastfeeding should be considered along with the mother''s clinical need for the drug.', '{"trimester_1": "Not established", "trimester_2": "Not established", "trimester_3": "Not established"}', 'Elobixibat acts locally in the gut by inhibiting the ileal bile acid transporter (IBAT), increasing bile acid concentration in the colon to stimulate fluid secretion and motility.', '["Elobixibat is administered orally and acts locally within the gastrointestinal tract with minimal systemic absorption.", "It selectively binds to and inhibits the ileal bile acid transporter (IBAT) located on the epithelial cells of the terminal ileum.", "This inhibition prevents the reabsorption of bile acids back into the enterohepatic circulation.", "Consequently, a larger volume of bile acids passes into the large intestinal lumen.", "In the colon, the increased concentration of bile acids promotes the secretion of water and electrolytes into the lumen and directly stimulates colonic smooth muscle motility, thereby relieving constipation."]', '**Bile Acid Sequestrants (e.g., Cholestyramine, Colesevelam):**
