- **Sulfite Sensitivity:** Some formulations may contain sulfites, which can cause allergic-type reactions, including anaphylaxis and life-threatening asthma episodes in susceptible individuals.
- **Metabolic Deficiencies:** Use with extreme caution in patients with decreased levels of plasma esterases (e.g., pseudocholinesterase deficiency), as tetracaine is an ester anesthetic and relies on these enzymes for metabolism.
- **Cardiovascular Disease:** Use caution in patients with heart block, rhythm disturbances, or shock, as local anesthetics can depress cardiac conductivity and contractility.
- **Special Populations:** Use caution in the elderly, debilitated patients, obstetrics, or patients with increased intraabdominal pressure.
- **Vasoconstrictor Use:** DO NOT use solutions containing epinephrine in distal areas of the body (e.g., digits, nose, ears, penis) due to the risk of profound ischemia and necrosis. Addition of a vasoconstrictor (epinephrine) in appropriate areas will promote local hemostasis, decrease systemic absorption, and increase the duration of action.', '- Spinal anesthesia (subarachnoid block) for surgical procedures requiring prolonged anesthesia (e.g., 2-3 hours).', '- **Hypersensitivity:** Contraindicated in patients with a known hypersensitivity to tetracaine, other ester-type local anesthetics, aminobenzoic acid (PABA) derivatives, sulfites, parabens, or any components of the preparation.
- **Spinal Anesthesia Contraindications:** Absolute contraindications to spinal anesthesia include severe hemorrhage, severe hypotension, shock, local infection at the site of proposed lumbar puncture, and septicemia.', '- **Central Nervous System:** Anxiety, restlessness, nervousness, dizziness, tremors, twitching, drowsiness, unconsciousness, seizures, respiratory arrest.
- **Cardiovascular:** Myocardial depression, hypotension, bradycardia, arrhythmias, cardiac arrest.
