Small amounts of contraceptive steroids are excreted in breast milk. Estrogen-containing oral contraceptives can reduce milk production in breastfeeding mothers. It is generally recommended to use alternative (progestin-only or non-hormonal) forms of contraception until the infant is fully weaned.', '{"trimester_1": "Contraindicated", "trimester_2": "Contraindicated", "trimester_3": "Contraindicated"}', 'This combination oral contraceptive suppresses the hypothalamic-pituitary system, preventing the mid-cycle luteinizing hormone (LH) surge and inhibiting ovulation, while also altering cervical mucus and the endometrium.', '["Ethinyl estradiol and gestodene provide continuous negative feedback to the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary gland.", "This feedback suppresses the secretion of Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone (GnRH), Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH), and Luteinizing Hormone (LH).", "The suppression of the LH surge prevents follicular maturation and ovulation.", "Additionally, the progestin (gestodene) thickens cervical mucus, creating a physical barrier that inhibits sperm penetration and motility.", "The hormones also alter the endometrial lining, making it thin and unreceptive to the implantation of a fertilized ovum."]', '- **CYP3A4 Inducers (e.g., Rifampin, Carbamazepine, Phenytoin, Barbiturates):** Accelerate the metabolism of contraceptive steroids, leading to breakthrough bleeding and contraceptive failure. Use a backup non-hormonal method.
- **Broad-Spectrum Antibiotics:** May alter intestinal flora, potentially reducing enterohepatic recirculation of estrogens and decreasing contraceptive efficacy.
