**Clostridium difficile-Associated Diarrhea:** Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) may increase the risk of *Clostridium difficile*-associated diarrhea (CDAD), especially in hospitalized patients. Consider CDAD in patients who develop persistent diarrhea.

**Bone Fracture:** Long-term and multiple daily dose PPI therapy may be associated with an increased risk for osteoporosis-related fractures of the hip, wrist, or spine.

**Hypomagnesemia:** Symptomatic and asymptomatic hypomagnesemia has been reported rarely in patients treated with PPIs for at least three months, and in most cases after a year of therapy. Monitor magnesium levels prior to initiation of prolonged treatment and periodically thereafter.

**Vitamin B12 Deficiency:** Daily treatment with any acid-suppressing medications over a long period of time (e.g., longer than 3 years) may lead to malabsorption of cyanocobalamin (Vitamin B12).', '- **Peptic Ulcer Disease:** For the healing and symptomatic relief of gastric and duodenal ulcers.
